5:## Individual deviations from normative electroencephalographic connectivity predict antidepressant response
21:Keywords: EEG Functional connectivity Individual deviation Major depressive disorder Antidepressant Treatment outcome
25:Major depressive disorder (MDD) is a prevalent psychiatric disorder with  a  lifetime  prevalence  of  over  20  %,  making  it  a  significant contributor  to  global  ill  health  and  disability  (Hasin  et  al.,  2018). However, the neuropathological mechanisms underlying MDD remain a
29:Background: Antidepressant  medications  yield  unsatisfactory  treatment  outcomes  in  patients  with  major depressive disorder (MDD) with modest advantages over the placebo, partly due to the elusive mechanisms of antidepressant responses and unexplained heterogeneity in patient's response to treatment. Here we develop a novel normative modeling framework to quantify individual deviations in psychopathological dimensions that offers a promising avenue for the personalized treatment for psychiatric disorders.
31:Methods: We built a normative model with resting-state electroencephalography (EEG) connectivity data from healthy controls of three independent cohorts. We characterized the individual deviation of MDD patients from the healthy norms, based on which we trained sparse predictive models for treatment responses of MDD patients (102  sertraline-medicated  and  119  placebo-medicated).  Hamilton  depression  rating  scale  (HAMD-17)  was assessed at both baseline and after the eight-week antidepressant treatment.
33:Results: We successfully predicted treatment outcomes for patients receiving sertraline ( r = 0.43, p &lt; 0.001) and placebo ( r = 0.33, p &lt; 0.001). We also showed that the normative modeling framework successfully distinguished subclinical and diagnostic variabilities among subjects. From the predictive models, we identified key connectivity signatures in resting-state EEG for antidepressant treatment, suggesting differences in neural circuit involvement between sertraline and placebo responses.
35:Conclusions: Our findings and highly generalizable framework advance the neurobiological understanding in the potential  pathways  of  antidepressant  responses,  enabling  more  targeted  and  effective  personalized  MDD treatment.
37:Trial  registration: Establishing Moderators and Biosignatures of Antidepressant Response for Clinical Care for Depression (EMBARC), NCT#01407094.
39:subject  of  debate  (Albert  et  al.,  2012;  Morrissette  and  Stahl,  2014; Wagstaff  et  al.,  2001;  Walker,  2013),  impeding  the  development  of effective antidepressants and leading to suboptimal treatment outcomes and  reduced  quality  of  life  for  individuals  with  MDD.  Although numerous medications have been approved for MDD treatment, their efficacy  beyond  placebo  is  modest  at  best,  with  only  a  few  newly
55:approved  medications  demonstrating  equivocal  effectiveness  (Fountoulakis  and  M ¨ oller,  2011;  Fournier  et  al.,  2010;  Khin  et  al.,  2011; Kirsch,  2019;  Kirsch  et  al.,  2008;  Turner  et  al.,  2008).  This  limited response rate is partly attributed to the heterogeneity among MDD patients, resulting in varied responses to antidepressant treatments (Carter et al., 2012; Cipriani et al., 2018). Additionally, the delayed onset of action of antidepressants, typically taking at least four weeks, means that MDD patients may spend months or even years searching for an effective treatment (Association, 2000) while enduring associated side effects (Levinstein and Samuels, 2014). Therefore, the ability to predict treatment efficacy early on could significantly expedite clinical decisionmaking  processes  and  improve  patient  outcomes.  Consequently,  a crucial step towards enhancing treatment outcomes and quality of life for individuals with MDD is the implementation of precision medicine, which involves utilizing pretreatment measures to quantify individual deviations  in  psychopathological  dimensions,  as  proposed  by  the Research Domain Criteria (RDoC) framework (Insel et al., 2010).
57:In the quest for precision medicine in psychiatric disorders, neuroimaging  techniques  have  demonstrated  remarkable  capabilities  in identifying  neurobiological  alterations  that  can  predict  disorder  diagnoses (Abi-Dargham and Horga, 2016; Dong et al., 2019; Houenou et al., 2012; Kl ¨ oppel et al., 2012; Kraguljac et al., 2021; Sun et al., 2018; Woo et al., 2017). However, previous neuroimaging studies have primarily  focused  on  group-level  differences  in  brain  features  between patients and healthy controls, overlooking the heterogeneity within the patient population. Consequently, efforts have been made to identify subtypes within each disorder (Beijers et al., 2019; Chang et al., 2021; Drysdale et al., 2017; Pelin et al., 2021; Zhang et al., 2021), with the goal of  tailoring  treatments  based  on  these  subtypes  to  achieve  precision medicine. Nevertheless, the translation of these subtype findings into improved clinical practice has been challenging, partially due to the inability to construct a continuous spectrum of psychopathological dimensions that accounts for heterogeneity within subtypes, as well as the dissociation between identified subtypes and treatment responses (Fava
59:Fig.  1. Flowchart  of  antidepressant  response  prediction  based  on  individual  deviations  from  normative  electroencephalographic  (EEG)  FC  (FC)  a  Normative modeling framework. First, a healthy norm was developed with healthy EEG FC using an autoencoder. Second, patient EEG FC were fed into the autoencoder, generating reconstructed EEG FC for each patient. The subject-level individual deviations from normative EEG FC were then quantified as the reconstruction error (raw FC - reconstructed FC) yielded by the autoencoder. b Distribution of individual deviations. The autoencoder captures common dimensions shared by healthy controls. The reconstruction errors, namely the individual deviations, reflect unseen dimensions in healthy controls. Those unseen dimensions were hypothesized to be associated with diseases, including MDD and other psychopathological dimensions. Thus, the individual deviations amplify the disease dimensions to yield FC features that are more predictive of treatment responses. c Predictive modeling framework. The individual deviations from normative EEG FC were utilized as the input features of the predictive modeling framework. First, correlation analyses were conducted on all individual deviations to identify statistically significant features of antidepressant responses. Second, insignificant features were masked out to reduce the feature dimensionality. Lastly, sparse learning techniques were implemented on selected features to develop cross-validated models and generate individual-level predictions of antidepressant responses. Response-predictive biomarkers were identified as feature weights of FC-based individual deviations.
65:et  al.,  1997).  To  address  these  challenges,  normative  modeling  has emerged as a promising framework for dissecting patient heterogeneity and conceptualizing psychopathological dimensions by quantifying individual deviations from a healthy norm (Marquand et al., 2019). While several  neuroimaging  studies  have  employed  normative  modeling, particularly  in  the  realm  of  structural  magnetic  resonance  imaging (MRI) (Marquand et al., 2016; Parkes et al., 2021), investigations utilizing  functional  connectivity  (FC)  features  remain  relatively  unexplored. Notably, our prior work utilizing resting-state functional MRI (rsfMRI) has demonstrated the potential of FC-based normative modeling  in  quantifying  individual  brain  dysfunction  and  parsing neurobiological heterogeneity (Maron-Katz et al., 2020; Wu et al., 2020; Zhao et al., 2023). Additionally, electroencephalography (EEG), another non-invasive  neuroimaging  technique,  provides  a  cost-effective  and user-friendly alternative to rsfMRI for clinical practice. Recent studies have shown that resting-state EEG (rsEEG) connectivity can facilitate MDD diagnosis (Duan et al., 2020) and reveal MDD subtypes (Zhang et al., 2021). Collectively, these recent research endeavors suggest that FC-based individual  deviations  hold  promise  in  providing  crucial  information regarding psychopathological dimensions, thereby paving the way towards precision medicine for MDD.
67:This study aimed to develop a normative modeling framework based on rsEEG FC to identify clinically translatable pretreatment biomarkers for antidepressant response prediction. To this end, we constructed an EEG FC-based normative modeling framework that quantifies individual deviations in psychopathological dimensions (Fig. 1) using three independent cohorts. First, employing an autoencoder-based reconstruction model,  we  established  a  healthy  norm  for  rsEEG  connectivity  that quantitatively measures individual deviations of MDD patients in psychopathological  dimensions.  Second,  to  tailor  these  individual  deviations to antidepressant responses, we  employed  a predictive modeling framework that captures treatment response-correlated individual  deviations  under  supervision,  leveraging  machine  learning techniques known for their promise in precision psychiatry (Chen et al., 2022).  Predictive  modeling  utilizing  the  quantified  FC  deviations enabled individual-level prediction of antidepressant responses. Subsequently,  we  confirmed  the  unique  advantage  of  the  individual  FC deviation-driven signatures by comparing the results with predictions obtained from commonly used clinical measures or raw EEG FC features. We also compared the performance of our autoencoder-based individual deviations  with  other  normative  modeling  strategies,  including  principal component analysis (PCA)-based and regression-based (Rutherford et al., 2022) individual deviations. Furthermore, to investigate whether sertraline and placebo treatment responses involve distinct neural circuits, we compared the importance of individual deviations in rsEEG connectivity  across  treatment  arms  and  brain  networks.  Finally,  we interpreted the autoencoder-based individual deviations using commonly used clinical measures. The present work aimed to generate translatable models for predicting antidepressant response using a novel and  interpretable  normative  modeling  framework,  thereby  enabling treatment outcome prediction and facilitating the realization of precision psychiatry for individuals with MDD.
73:Overall, we included healthy controls from three independent cohorts and MDD patients from one clinical cohort with antidepressant treatment response data. We integrated three cohorts of 154 healthy controls to ensure our healthy norms represent inter-cohort variability and improve the generalizability of our findings. We focused antidepressant response predictive modeling on one cohort of 221 medicated MDD patients  (aged  18 -65)  because  of  the  availability  of  treatment outcome data. A brief summary of subjects included in our study can be found in Supplementary Table 1 and Supplementary methods.
81:To ensure the individual deviation of each subject was independent of other subjects, the normalization of EEG FC data was conducted on each subject. For each subject, we first performed an inverse hyperbolic tangent  transformation  on  the  EEG  FC  data  to  better  accommodate deviated values of EEG FC. The transformed strengths of 4950 FCs were then z-scored to have a zero mean and unit standard deviation. Notably, this procedure also guaranteed that all the three cohorts had the same FC mean and standard deviation. This normalization strategy endowed the EEG FC of each subject with the independence of other subjects and emphasized the distinguishability between brain regions with high and low FCs.
85:Above all, to calibrate demographics across the independent cohorts, thus to improve the reliability and prediction performance of normative modeling,  we  developed  healthy  norms  of  154  young  adults  (age 18 -25), which consist of 8 subjects from the EMBARC cohort, 75 subjects from the depression-EEG cohort, and 71 subjects from the LEMON cohort. The age threshold of 25 was chosen because the Depression-EEG cohort only had subjects younger than 22, and the LEMON cohort only recorded subjects' age with a resolution of 5 years. Therefore, including healthy controls with age between 18 and 25 was the best way to match demographics of the three independent cohorts. Healthy norms were derived using three strategies as discussed below.
93:dimensionality. Here we established the PCA-based reconstruction error to demonstrate that a linear unsupervised reconstruction might be too simple  to  capture  individual  deviation  information.  Specifically,  we applied a PCA on the 4950 EEG FCs of healthy subjects and empirically selected the first 100 principal components (explained &gt; 90 % variance) to reduce dimensionality. The reconstructed FCs were calculated as the matrix product of kept principal components and transposed eigenvectors. The reconstruction error, which was subsequently used to develop antidepressant response prediction models, was calculated as the difference between original FCs and reconstructed FCs.
97:Afterward,  we  calculated  individual  deviations  using  an  existing normative modeling framework. A recent study developed a comprehensive and user-friendly workflow for realizing normative modeling in precision psychiatry (Rutherford et al., 2022). We employed this standardized framework to derive regression-based individual deviations of rsEEG FCs. Briefly, the framework requires users to pre-select a set of covariates and then regress out those covariates based on regression models for FCs. Specifically, we included age and gender as covariates (they were the only two demographic variables shared by all three independent cohorts)  and  calculated  individual  deviations  in  FCs  with reference to the age and gender-based FC prediction, which was trained on healthy subjects and applied on MDD patients. The resulting individual deviations were then used to develop antidepressant response prediction models, which unfortunately yielded unsatisfactory prediction performance. Notably, the unsatisfactory performance of regression-based individual deviations in antidepressant response prediction might be due to the lack of common variables that were usable as covariates.  It  might  be  also  because  the  regression  models  were  not accurate enough to robustly estimate individual deviations in FCs. But in either case, it demonstrated the approach's over-reliance on pre-selected covariates  and  regression  performance,  calling  for  an  unsupervised normative modeling strategy that is more flexible and accommodating.
101:The  autoencoder  has  been  employed  by  numerous  neuroimaging studies as the normative modeling framework (Kumar and Sotiras, 2021; Pinaya et al., 2019; Pinaya et al., 2021) for its independence of supervision  and  high  flexibility.  The  autoencoder  we  employed  for  the healthy norm development consists of three layers, including an input layer, a hidden layer, and an output layer. The input and output layers have a dimensionality of 4950, which is the total number of FCs with a 100-ROI  brain  atlas  (Schaefer  et  al.,  2017).  The  hidden  layer  has  a reduced dimensionality to allow the autoencoder to find representative dimensions for the healthy controls (Fig. 1). We empirically selected a dimensionality of 500 for the hidden layer to reduce the dimensionality magnitude while keeping adequate complexity of autoencoders.

